fact WorkItem(id: "WI-801", description: some(value: "The typer and eval pivot the spread-vs-whole decision on DIFFERENT QUANTITIES at a Function[A,B,E] slot, and nothing checks the two against each other. WI-788's positional_arg_expectations (kb/typing.rs) reads A's COMPONENT COUNT to decide that N arguments are A's components spread across the call; eval's spread_eta_args and gather_closure_arg (eval/eval.rs) pivot on the CALLEE's OWN arity (params.len() / Pattern::binder_arity). They coincide exactly when the callback conforms to Function[A, ...], which a separate conformance check establishes. If that conformance ever admits a callback whose binder count differs from A's arity, the typer ACCEPTS the call and eval traps ArityMismatch -- the load-clean-then-trap class WI-782/791/792/788 exist to remove. The codebase already names this hazard and owns a mechanism for it: Pattern::binder_arity (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~859) is documented as 'ONE definition, deliberately shared by the two sides that must agree on it... a disagreement is not a cosmetic drift -- it is a program that typechecks and then traps at eval'. The new typer site is a THIRD party to that agreement and is not enrolled; WI-788 added a doc cross-link naming spread_eta_args as its dynamic twin, which is a marker, not a fix. FIX: a shared classify_function_application(param_ty, supplied) both sides call, or an equivalent single owner of the quantity. Found by /code-review on WI-788. ACCEPTANCE: one owner for the spread decision, called by typer and eval; a test constructs a callback whose binder count differs from A's arity and asserts a LOAD error rather than an eval trap; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T20:00:56Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-801", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED with a NARROWER rule than this ticket's stated acceptance, deliberately. The ticket asked for a LOAD error when a callback's 'binder count differs from A's arity'. That is too broad: it would refuse arity ONE, which is the CANONICAL inhabitant of the declared type -- 'lambda t -> t._1' simply IS an '(Int64, Int64) -> Int64'. docs/kernel-language.md (the 'Function states no arity' note) says a 'Function[(A,B),R]' 'accepts either' a single-tuple-argument callback OR a two-parameter one, so the admitted set is exactly {1, |A|}. Implemented that. MEASUREMENT also found a SECOND defect the ticket does not name: the spread call form 'f(1,2)' trapped against an arity-1 callee ('ArityMismatch expected 1 got 2') even though the spec makes both call forms legal at the slot. Only the whole-tuple form worked in every cell. That one cannot be fixed in eval -- a gather needs A's component LABELS, erased at runtime -- so the TYPER now normalizes a spread call into its whole-A tuple, and eval's two existing adapters are unchanged. All 16 matrix cells (positional/named A x whole/spread callee x whole/spread call, lambda and operation) now load and evaluate. TWO EXISTING TESTS CHANGED STAGE, not invariant: wi784::nullary_thunk_called_with_an_argument and wi785::arity_mismatch_still_refuses_to_match both documented themselves as pinning a runtime-detection corner and now refuse at LOAD; wi784::a_callback_arity_the_typer_cannot_decide_still_fails_in_the_matcher was added so the eval-stage matcher guard keeps coverage via a rigid A. /code-review additionally found the new arity diagnostic was installed on 1 of 3 channels (op-arg only); let-annotation and op-return now route through the same renderer.", at: "2026-07-21T20:01:21Z")
